WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.720
From reincarnation to run-ins&nbsp;
with the Mafia and the FBI,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.720 --> 00:00:08.880
Steven Seagal has lived quite the&nbsp;
explosive life both on screen and off.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.700
Before Steven Seagal became a major star on the&nbsp;
success of macho action flicks like Marked for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:19.440
Death and Out for Justice, he had a bit of an&nbsp;
inspirational life story. Born in Michigan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.520
he moved with his parents to California,&nbsp;
where the frail and asthmatic young Steven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:28.920
became obsessed with rock and roll and martial&nbsp;
arts. He learned aikido under the tutelage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.540
an older man of Japanese descent, then spent&nbsp;
some time in Japan before returning to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.040
U.S. to open a chain of successful dojos.&nbsp;
His story was so ready-made for Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.480
that the movie industry had already&nbsp;
told versions of it. Several of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:47.520
biographical details bear a strong, albeit&nbsp;
coincidental, resemblance to The Karate Kid.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:52.980
Even if Seagal's journey to becoming a warrior was&nbsp;
Hollywood fodder, it wasn't necessarily the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:57.660
he wanted to project. Often in interviews, he&nbsp;
would foreground his family's roots in Brooklyn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.560
presenting himself as a kid from the&nbsp;
block who made good. But that particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:06.360
bubble was burst in a 1990 People Magazine&nbsp;
profile, in which his mother Pam clarified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:11.580
that his Brooklyn upbringing was limited to&nbsp;
a handful of family visits. She also noted,

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.960
"He was a puny kid back then."

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:17.940
The People profile punctured Seagal's&nbsp;
self-promoted image as a New York kid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.380
and took aim at one of his tales about his&nbsp;
pre-Hollywood life. Seagal claimed that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:26.940
while living in Osaka in the 1970s&nbsp;
with his first wife Miyako Fujitani,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:32.520
he performed training and consulting work for&nbsp;
the CIA. This bit of unverified history began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:37.200
circulating in 1988, during the promotion&nbsp;
of his feature debut, Above the Law. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:42.240
film's plot follows rogue CIA operatives, and&nbsp;
Seagal claimed a certain amount of expertise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:47.820
in the field and even suggested that the script&nbsp;
came from his own experiences as a company man.

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:52.380
Fujitani couldn't speak about her husband's&nbsp;
clandestine activities during their ten-year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:56.520
marriage, although she did confirm to People that&nbsp;
there were long stretches of time when he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.620
unavailable. She also noted that while he could&nbsp;
have been guarding the Shah of Iran or Bishop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:07.320
Desmond Tutu, as he claimed, it's just as likely&nbsp;
that he was a poor husband and absentee father.

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:11.340
In the early 90s, Seagal hired retired&nbsp;
intelligence officer Herbert Saunders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:16.680
for security detail. Then in 2003, Saunders&nbsp;
confirmed to the New York Post that Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.360
pressed him to use his CIA abilities and&nbsp;
access to do everything from stealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.920
files from the Drug Enforcement Agency&nbsp;
to killing someone. As Saunders put it,

00:02:26.096 --> 00:02:28.436
"I don't think he's able to&nbsp;
sort out fantasy from fact."

00:02:28.501 --> 00:02:31.122
"There are an awful lot of people&nbsp;
who say that that's not true."

00:02:31.122 --> 00:02:36.060
By the early 80s, Seagal was busy with&nbsp;
his aikido dojos in Osaka, Los Angeles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.940
and Taos, New Mexico. The North Hollywood location&nbsp;
quickly became the talk of the town, and in 1986,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:47.520
the Los Angeles Times wrote a glowing story&nbsp;
about Seagal, generally taking his bold claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:52.320
about his mastery of aikido and Japanese culture&nbsp;
at face value. The paper reported that his time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.660
in Osaka proved his mettle as a martial artist&nbsp;
and his disdain for impostor karate teachers.

00:02:57.660 --> 00:03:03.000
Seagal's dojo attracted film industry&nbsp;
luminaries like James Mason and James Coburn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.620
but his most significant student was someone&nbsp;
less well-known to the public at large:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:14.880
Hollywood super-agent and future Disney president&nbsp;
Michael Ovitz. In 1987, Ovitz arranged a martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:19.620
arts demonstration for a group of Warner Brothers&nbsp;
executives. While film studios had been sniffing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.620 --> 00:03:25.020
around Seagal for a few years, Ovitz was the one&nbsp;
who finally made it happen. He arranged for Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:30.360
to star in Above the Law, and helped him get&nbsp;
story and producer credits on the film as well.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:36.780
Along with Ovitz, other Hollywood notables shaped&nbsp;
Seagal's career at this early stage — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.400
British actress and model Kelly LeBrock,&nbsp;
who had captivated audiences in the 80s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:46.080
in such films as The Woman in Red and Weird&nbsp;
Science. She and Seagal met in Tokyo, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.700
she was shooting a fashion campaign, and their&nbsp;
relationship gave him some status that he might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:56.280
not have enjoyed otherwise. The 1986 Los Angeles&nbsp;
Times profile of Seagal even included the claim,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.080
"Kelly LeBrock [...] apparently was&nbsp;
his reward for years of self denial."

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:06.180
"Years of self-denial" is an odd way to&nbsp;
describe a decade of marriage to his first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.260
wife that produced two children, but the&nbsp;
profile makes no mention of them. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:15.540
Seagal had been married twice beforehand: in&nbsp;
1984, he married actress Adrienne La Russa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.100
despite still being legally married to Miyako&nbsp;
Fujitani. Within a year, La Russa annulled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.480
their marriage, and by 1986, Fujitani&nbsp;
divorced him as well. As for LeBrock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:30.480
she and Seagal married in 1987, but their&nbsp;
union ended less than amicably in 1996.

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.340
Over the course of his career, Steven Seagal&nbsp;
has done quite a bit of fight choreography.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.820
Most of the films he's choreographed&nbsp;
are his own — but in the early 80s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.740
he worked on the James Bond film Never Say&nbsp;
Never Again, training Sean Connery in martial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:49.200
arts. Connery was already well-versed in karate:&nbsp;
while preparing for 1967's You Only Live Twice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:54.240
he was even awarded an honorary third-degree&nbsp;
black belt. But for his seventh outing as Bond, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.100
needed to learn a little aikido, so Seagal taught&nbsp;
him how to throw opponents and manipulate joints.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.800
At first, the lessons seemed to be going well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.660
but then Connery got a little cocky… so an&nbsp;
irritated Seagal allegedly grabbed his arm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.880
and broke the actor's wrist. While Connery&nbsp;
was undoubtedly in pain, he reportedly kept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.280
training and didn't even realize the&nbsp;
extent of his injury until years later.

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:17.040
"15 years ago."

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:17.760
"And it's still broken?"

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.234
"Yeah, I just found out last year."

00:05:19.260 --> 00:05:25.080
In his heyday, Steven Seagal was the biggest&nbsp;
martial arts star in Hollywood — but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.640
doesn't exactly mean that he could fight in&nbsp;
real life. He may be a seventh dan in aikido,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:34.380
a martial art that relies on joint locks and the&nbsp;
redirection of momentum — but aikido is pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.520
worthless when it comes to self-defense.&nbsp;
According to fight analyst Jack Slack,

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:42.360
"Aikido [...] famously only works if the&nbsp;
opponent is running straight at you."

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:47.700
Alas, this is something most smart fighters&nbsp;
never do — but nevertheless, Seagal claimed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:53.400
that he could beat anyone, anywhere, anytime.&nbsp;
Furthermore, he made some controversial comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:59.940
about Bruce Lee, Chuck Norris, and full-contact&nbsp;
karate. This didn't sit well with Bob Wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:05.100
an actor and high-ranking black belt with&nbsp;
plenty of tournament experience. Wall was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:09.900
friends with Norris, had worked with Lee,&nbsp;
and didn't much care for Seagal's attitude.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:15.480
That was when Wall assembled a group of kickboxing&nbsp;
and karate champions dubbed the "Dirty Dozen."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:21.060
Some of them wanted to expose Seagal as a fake,&nbsp;
while others wanted to avenge some stuntmen he'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:25.380
allegedly injured. To get his attention,&nbsp;
the Dozen appeared in various magazines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:30.900
but there was ultimately never any physical&nbsp;
showdown. When Seagal finally met Wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:33.840
the movie star reportedly&nbsp;
apologized for what he'd said.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:40.208
Seagal has battled all sorts of bad guys on the&nbsp;
silver screen, from terrorists to the yakuza. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.208 --> 00:06:45.240
But he's also faced some villains in real life,&nbsp;
including the Mafia. In the 90s, the actor's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:50.340
partnership with producer Julius R. Nasso resulted&nbsp;
in a string of hits. Alas, their relationship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:55.320
soured in 2000, and things took a dark turn&nbsp;
when the Gambino crime family got involved.

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:58.680
One day, Seagal was reportedly&nbsp;
escorted to a Brooklyn restaurant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:03.540
where he met an alleged Gambino captain named&nbsp;
Anthony "Sonny" Ciccone. According to Seagal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.540 --> 00:07:08.460
Ciccone ordered him to start working with&nbsp;
Nasso again and demanded that Seagal fork over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.800
$150,000 for every film he made.&nbsp;
Evidently, Seagal was so shaken that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:19.140
he gave the gangsters $700,000. As he left&nbsp;
the meeting, someone supposedly told him,

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:21.960
"If you would have said the wrong&nbsp;
thing, they would have killed you."

00:07:21.960 --> 00:07:26.100
The scandal came to light in 2003, when&nbsp;
the government indicted alleged crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:31.680
boss Peter Gotti and 16 others for a host of&nbsp;
crimes. In addition to Seagal's testimony,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.300
the government had recordings of several&nbsp;
individuals, including Ciccone and Nasso,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:41.340
discussing how they would intimidate Seagal.&nbsp;
They even laughed about how they scared the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:46.380
movie star. Nasso defended himself by saying&nbsp;
Seagal had backed out of several movie deals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:51.840
owed him $500,000. He was sentenced to one year&nbsp;
in federal prison, though he still sued Seagal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:57.660
for $60 million. After Nasso got out of jail, he&nbsp;
settled with the actor for an undisclosed amount.

00:07:57.801 --> 00:07:59.241
"I still know a thing or two."

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:03.600
While never a critical darling, Seagal&nbsp;
used to pull in some impressive box&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.200
office numbers. These days, though,&nbsp;
Seagal's films tend to go straight to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:11.460
video on demand — and he blames his&nbsp;
decline on, of all things, the FBI.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:16.140
In 2002, Los Angeles Times reporter Anita&nbsp;
Busch was investigating Seagal and Julius&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:21.300
Nasso. One morning, she found a dead fish,&nbsp;
a rose, and a sign that read "Stop!" on her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.260
windshield. She informed the FBI, who&nbsp;
began investigating Seagal. At first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.580
the feds thought that he'd hired a detective&nbsp;
named Anthony Pellicano to intimidate Busch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:34.740
They also suspected Seagal of hiring a thug to&nbsp;
scare Vanity Fair writer Ned Zeman with a gun.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:39.900
But after digging deeper, the FBI concluded that&nbsp;
there was no evidence against Seagal. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.680
they focused on Pellicano, the private&nbsp;
eye who illegally spied on celebrities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:49.620
The FBI never charged Seagal, but they didn't&nbsp;
exactly exonerate him either — and he believes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:53.460
all the notoriety from the Pellicano&nbsp;
case significantly harmed his career.

00:08:53.880 --> 00:08:58.980
It may not surprise you to learn that Steven&nbsp;
Seagal is fascinated with law enforcement. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:04.080
even served as a cop in Jefferson Parish,&nbsp;
Louisiana for about 20 years. In 2009, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:09.540
actor took things to the next level by starring&nbsp;
in a reality show called Steven Seagal: Lawman.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:13.680
In the third season of the show, Seagal&nbsp;
moved to Arizona and teamed up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:19.080
controversial Sheriff Joe Arpaio of Maricopa&nbsp;
County. Arpaio is an infamous media hound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:24.180
so when Seagal showed up with a camera in tow, the&nbsp;
sheriff decided to put on a very memorable show.

00:09:24.180 --> 00:09:28.260
In 2011, Arpaio orchestrated&nbsp;
a raid against Jesus Llovera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:33.660
a local man suspected of cockfighting. When&nbsp;
the cops arrived, they had more than just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:39.480
warrant. There were up to 40 SWAT officers,&nbsp;
a bomb squad, K-9 units, armored vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.480 --> 00:09:45.180
and even Seagal riding on top of a tank. During&nbsp;
the raid, parts of the suspect's house were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:49.920
damaged, as Seagal's tank tore down the gates&nbsp;
to Llovera's property. After arresting Llovera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:55.440
Seagal and Maricopa officials discovered that&nbsp;
Llovera owned over 100 roosters, which they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.440 --> 00:10:00.720
decided to euthanize. Llovera even claimed that&nbsp;
the police also killed his 11-month-old puppy.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:05.157
"Animal cruelty is one of my, uh, pet peeves."

00:10:05.157 --> 00:10:08.880
Llovera started to file a lawsuit against&nbsp;
the Maricopa County Sheriff's Office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.720
demanded an apology letter from Seagal —&nbsp;
but after pleading guilty to cockfighting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.328
the lawsuit was dropped. As for the&nbsp;
raid, it ultimately never aired on TV.

00:10:17.340 --> 00:10:23.700
As someone who's so famous for breaking bones,&nbsp;
Steven Seagal is surprisingly gentle towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.700 --> 00:10:30.420
animals. He's both a vegetarian and something&nbsp;
of an activist. In 1999, Seagal worked to force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:35.520
South Africa to stop exporting baby elephants&nbsp;
to Japan and received a PETA Humanitarian Award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:39.480
for his efforts. Four years later, he wrote to&nbsp;
the government of Thailand to get them to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:44.100
torturing baby elephants. He's also attempted&nbsp;
to shame India into treating cows better.

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:50.040
But Seagal's favorite animals of all are of the&nbsp;
soothsaying variety. During an interview with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.040 --> 00:10:54.480
PETA, he claimed that while he was learning&nbsp;
aikido in Japan, a white dog greeted him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.480 --> 00:10:59.460
After a few days, the canine started barking,&nbsp;
apparently telepathically warning him that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:04.740
his dojo was on fire. Seagal put the fire out,&nbsp;
and the dog disappeared, never to bark again.

00:11:05.135 --> 00:11:07.235
"Cause I'm an animal lover,&nbsp;
you know? 'Animal lover'?"

00:11:07.235 --> 00:11:08.134
"Yeah."
"Yeah."

00:11:08.700 --> 00:11:13.920
Seagal's hands aren't just for busting up bodies.&nbsp;
They're also for making sweet, sweet music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:19.620
as he is a long-time musician who loves himself&nbsp;
some blues. Seagal has been playing guitar since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:25.740
age 12, and he's the frontman of the aptly&nbsp;
named Steven Seagal Blues Band. In 2005,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.740 --> 00:11:31.140
he released his first album, entitled Songs From&nbsp;
the Crystal Cave. Overall, it's a straightforward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.140 --> 00:11:37.560
blues record, not nearly as grimy and soulful as&nbsp;
the likes of B.B. King or Buddy Guy. SputnikMusic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:43.080
wasn't exactly impressed, calling it "the Plan&nbsp;
9 From Outer Space of records" in their review.

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:47.460
We're inclined to agree with that take,&nbsp;
as the track entitled "Music" barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:52.920
qualifies as music. Meanwhile, "Girl, It's&nbsp;
Alright" sounds like the first song that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.420
Jack Johnson threw in the trash. And then&nbsp;
there's "Jealousy," a hungry ghost song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:02.083
that could maybe serve as inspiration&nbsp;
for a high school yearbook quote tune.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:07.140
Energy drinks boomed in the mid-2000s.&nbsp;
With the success of Red Bull and Monster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:12.180
dozens of competitors flooded the market, and&nbsp;
they were often endorsed by celebrities. A certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.180 --> 00:12:17.580
action star got in on the game in 2005 with the&nbsp;
release of Steven Seagal's Lightning Bolt, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:23.700
ginseng and goji berry-powered drink available in&nbsp;
two flavors: Cherry Charge and Asian Experience.

00:12:23.700 --> 00:12:29.640
Seagal released a baffling two-minute commercial&nbsp;
featuring himself and swimsuit models hanging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:34.560
out by a pool. The premise was that he was so&nbsp;
enamored by his own beverage that he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:40.560
swim in it. It's hard to decide which element of&nbsp;
the ad is more offensive: ingredients with names&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.560 --> 00:12:46.860
like "Tibetan Goji" and "Asian Cordyceps," the&nbsp;
models being unable to fake any chemistry with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:52.320
Seagal, or the nondescript blues guitar score.&nbsp;
Reviews of the drink were less than appetizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:57.034
though cans are still available through eBay if&nbsp;
you really want to try them after all these years.

00:12:57.034 --> 00:13:01.218
"Lightning Bolt: try it today. It's electric."

00:13:01.218 --> 00:13:06.360
Russian President Vladimir Putin has a reputation&nbsp;
as a dictator and human rights violator,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.360 --> 00:13:10.920
but Steven Seagal doesn't exactly see him&nbsp;
that way. He's even been quoted as saying —

00:13:10.920 --> 00:13:14.820
"I know him well enough to know that he&nbsp;
is one of the greatest world leaders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:17.700
if not the greatest world leader, alive today."

00:13:17.700 --> 00:13:22.740
The two met in 2003 at the Moscow Film Festival&nbsp;
and quickly hit it off — probably thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:27.420
their shared love of martial arts, as Putin&nbsp;
is an eighth-degree black belt in judo. Soon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.420 --> 00:13:32.280
they were dining together, visiting dojos,&nbsp;
and promoting old Soviet exercise programs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:36.120
They even visited the Russian judo team&nbsp;
during preparation for the 2012 Olympics.

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:41.880
In 2015, Putin asked President Barack Obama&nbsp;
to make Seagal an honorary consul of Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:46.500
but Obama vetoed that idea pretty quickly.&nbsp;
Despite that, Seagal visits Russia frequently.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:51.480
He has given aikido demonstrations, visited&nbsp;
the factory that produces Kalashnikov rifles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:55.560
attended a parade on the 70th anniversary&nbsp;
of the Nazi surrender to the Soviet Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.520
and played a concert for pro-Russian&nbsp;
separatists in the Crimean Peninsula.

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:05.700
In November 2016, Seagal officially received&nbsp;
a Russian passport and became a citizen of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.700 --> 00:14:10.200
the country. What's more, Putin personally&nbsp;
signed the passport and expressed his hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:14.640
that the stunt would promote a normalization of&nbsp;
relations between the United States and Russia.

00:14:14.640 --> 00:14:19.800
But it appears that this new Russian citizen&nbsp;
is already grinding other countries' gears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.960
as it was announced in May 2017 that Seagal&nbsp;
would be banned from entering Ukraine for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:29.640
five years. According to the Ukraine Security&nbsp;
Service, the ban was implemented "on the basis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.840
of Ukrainian national security," likely&nbsp;
due to Seagal's admiration for Russia's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:38.100
aggressive tactics in the region. There was no&nbsp;
word on whether his movies were banned as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.100 --> 00:14:41.493
though we have to imagine he's not winning&nbsp;
any popularity contests over there.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:45.720
In 2023, Steven Seagal received&nbsp;
the Russian Order of Friendship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.720 --> 00:14:50.940
a civilian honor available to both citizens&nbsp;
and non-citizens of Russia. The year prior,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:56.220
a holding company co-owned by Seagal earned&nbsp;
a parcel of land outside Moscow to build a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.220 --> 00:15:01.440
martial arts center. The area had reportedly been&nbsp;
previously set aside for a children's hospital.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:05.460
Seagal's cozy relationship with Putin&nbsp;
aside, the Order of Friendship is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:09.420
empty gesture. Established by former&nbsp;
president Boris Yeltsin in 1994,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.420 --> 00:15:14.100
the recognition has been bestowed on dozens&nbsp;
of political and cultural figures. Past&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:18.780
honorees have included chess master Anatoly&nbsp;
Karpov, orchestra conductor Riccardo Muti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:24.600
and Queen Elizabeth II'scousin, Prince Michael&nbsp;
of Kent, who gave his medal back in protest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:29.760
the war in Ukraine. Canadian politician Adrienne&nbsp;
Clarkson and Norwegian politician Rune Rafaelsen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:33.240
have also both returned their Orders of&nbsp;
Friendship since the start of the war.

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:39.350
"Russia and America should be great&nbsp;
allies, and that's the way it should be."

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:44.640
Steven Seagal is a man of contrasts. He's&nbsp;
a high-profile Hollywood Buddhist and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:47.940
supporter of environmental causes,&nbsp;
while simultaneously a champion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.940 --> 00:15:52.560
of strongman regimes and corresponding&nbsp;
paranoid reactionary politics. In 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.560 --> 00:15:57.120
Seagal took his first dip into literary&nbsp;
waters with the release of his debut book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.120 --> 00:16:02.280
The Way of the Shadow Wolves: The Deep State&nbsp;
and the Hijacking of America. The book was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:07.380
co-written by Arizona politician Tom Morrissey&nbsp;
and includes a foreword by Sheriff Joe Arpaio.

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:12.060
The story is a hodgepodge of Trump-era&nbsp;
conspiracies surrounding the Deep State,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:15.960
the supposed group of unelected&nbsp;
bureaucrats plotting against the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:20.580
American people from deep within the federal&nbsp;
government. Their master plan is to smuggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.580 --> 00:16:24.300
Middle Eastern terrorists through the&nbsp;
Mexican border, but standing in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.300 --> 00:16:30.300
way is Tribal officer and shameless Seagal&nbsp;
avatar John Nan Tan Gode. He's a Mohawk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.300 --> 00:16:35.100
or an Apache, depending on the chapter.&nbsp;
He's also a member of the Shadow Wolves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:40.020
a real-life Indigenous patrol unit that works&nbsp;
under the Department of Homeland Security.

00:16:40.020 --> 00:16:46.200
Seagal and Morrisey depict the Shadow Wolves as&nbsp;
so morally pure and in harmony with the land that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.200 --> 00:16:51.900
they have nearly supernatural abilities. The&nbsp;
mysticism would be offensive if the writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:56.880
wasn't so laughably bad. Curious minds can&nbsp;
still find the novel available for purchase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:01.740
but you might be better off reading one of&nbsp;
the book's many bewildered reviews instead.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:07.920
Steven Seagal is willing to lend his name and&nbsp;
image to some dicey projects, and there's perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:13.740
no riskier example in his personal history than&nbsp;
the cryptocurrency bonanza of the late 2010s and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.740 --> 00:17:20.040
early 2020s. In the first quarter of 2018, Seagal&nbsp;
went to bat for a project called Bitcoiin2gen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:25.020
also known as B2G. He promoted the coin&nbsp;
on his social media pages and allowed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:29.280
likeness to appear on official branding,&nbsp;
without disclosing that he'd been promised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:36.540
$250,000 in cash and another $750,000 in B2G&nbsp;
tokens for his endorsement. If you're an expert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:41.220
in white-collar crime, you'll know that this&nbsp;
is a violation of federal anti-touting law.

00:17:41.220 --> 00:17:46.260
In 2020, the Securities and Exchange Commission&nbsp;
came down hard on Seagal for his violations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.260 --> 00:17:53.880
issuing fines of $157,000 in disgorgement and&nbsp;
another $157,000 in penalties. Seagal paid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.660 --> 00:17:59.100
$75,000 towards that total and then fled&nbsp;
to Russia, hoping that his status as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:04.200
Russian citizen and close friendship with Putin&nbsp;
would keep the rest of his money safe. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:08.520
in 2021 a federal judge ruled that&nbsp;
the SEC was still owed the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:13.020
penalty and could collect it through&nbsp;
Seagal's business manager. As for B2G,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:19.439
founder John DeMarr faced fraud charges that led&nbsp;
to a five-year prison sentence in January 2023.

00:18:19.439 --> 00:18:23.400
According to multiple actors that he's&nbsp;
worked with, Steven Seagal isn't exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:28.080
the most pleasant co-star. For example,&nbsp;
while filming 1996's Executive Decision,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.960
he allegedly shoved John Leguizamo against a wall.

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:35.640
"He caught me off guard and knocked all&nbsp;
the air out of me. I was like, 'Why? Why?'"

00:18:35.640 --> 00:18:40.620
Seagal also made life hard for the&nbsp;
Saturday Night Live cast when he hosted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:45.480
an episode in April 1991. As Tim Meadows&nbsp;
revealed in the book Live from New York,

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:47.580
"He didn't realize that you&nbsp;
can't tell somebody they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:47.580 --> 00:18:51.000
stupid on Wednesday and expect them to&nbsp;
continue writing for you on Saturday."

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:53.880
And according to fellow cast member Julia Sweeney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:56.940
Seagal wanted to perform a sketch&nbsp;
where he played a therapist who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.940 --> 00:19:02.220
wants to sleep with a rape survivor. He was&nbsp;
reportedly banned from ever hosting again.

00:19:02.220 --> 00:19:10.140
"Steven Seagal was so horrible on such a scale,&nbsp;
it was such a huge scale of terribleness."

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:15.120
In 1996's The Glimmer Man, Stephen Tobolowsky&nbsp;
played a serial killer who was supposed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:19.620
defeated by Seagal's character, but Seagal had&nbsp;
decided that it was bad karma to keep killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:24.240
people on-screen. Tobolowsky explained that his&nbsp;
character was trapped in his own private hell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:28.020
and by killing the villain, Seagal would&nbsp;
be allowing the bad guy to reincarnate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:33.000
as a more peaceful being. Seagal agreed, and&nbsp;
the scene went on as scripted. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.000 --> 00:19:38.040
Seagal later ad-libbed the line "Thank God,&nbsp;
I didn't kill that guy." Tobolowsky then had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.040 --> 00:19:41.580
to record a few lines to make it seem&nbsp;
like his obviously dead character had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.580 --> 00:19:46.034
somehow survived. However, those lines&nbsp;
ultimately didn't make the final cut.

00:19:46.500 --> 00:19:51.600
In 1997, Penor Rinpoche, the Supreme Head&nbsp;
of the Nyingma School of Tibetan Buddhism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.600 --> 00:19:58.020
announced that Steven Seagal was a reincarnated&nbsp;
Buddhist master. Rinpoche asserted that in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.020 --> 00:20:04.200
17th century, Seagal was Terton Chungdrag&nbsp;
Dorje, a renowned translator who opened a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:04.200 --> 00:20:09.120
monastery and found several powerful relics.&nbsp;
Thanks to this supposed spiritual history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:09.120 --> 00:20:13.620
Seagal was declared a lama, which&nbsp;
is a venerated teacher in Tibetan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.620 --> 00:20:17.820
Buddhism. During his inauguration, Seagal&nbsp;
pledged to help ease suffering across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:22.380
globe — and since then, he's given seminars&nbsp;
on compassion at New Age retreat centers.

00:20:22.380 --> 00:20:28.380
However, several people are skeptical of Seagal's&nbsp;
spiritual status. Since he's been accused of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.380 --> 00:20:33.060
sexual harassment, many have questioned&nbsp;
his moral character. Others allege that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:33.060 --> 00:20:38.280
before he was declared a Buddhist master, Seagal&nbsp;
donated quite a bit of cash to Rinpoche's school,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:43.500
which might explain his conveniently&nbsp;
glorious past life. It's all especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.500 --> 00:20:48.720
abnormal considering that these reincarnated&nbsp;
teachers are generally discovered as children.

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:53.940
In 2018, Emmy-winning actress Julianna&nbsp;
Margulies was a guest on The Katie Couric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:58.620
Podcast. The ladies discussed the #MeToo&nbsp;
movement, and Margulies recalled a hotel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:02.760
room meeting she'd had with Steven Seagal&nbsp;
in the early 90s. When a casting director&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:05.700
told her that Seagal wanted to go&nbsp;
over a scene with her in his room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.700 --> 00:21:10.320
she went, under the assumption that the casting&nbsp;
director would also attend. As she recalled,

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:14.400
"I walked in and I sat down and I&nbsp;
jumped right back up because there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.400 --> 00:21:18.300
was something very uncomfortable and hard in&nbsp;
the couch. He laughed and said, 'Oh, sorry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:22.020
that must have been my gun. He lifted&nbsp;
up the cushion and he took out his gun."

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:24.300
When Margulies grew nervous, Seagal explained,

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:27.960
"Oh, it's just for all the crazies that&nbsp;
are out there. I have to protect myself."

00:21:27.960 --> 00:21:33.060
Seagal also claimed to be a "healer" and&nbsp;
asked to read Margulies's palms. He told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:33.060 --> 00:21:37.140
her that she had weak kidneys — and shortly&nbsp;
thereafter, she managed to get out of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.140 --> 00:21:40.680
room. But then she realized that she hadn't&nbsp;
received the cab fare she'd been promised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:45.420
so she went back and asked for it. She got the&nbsp;
cash, as well as the part in Out for Justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:49.043
but she also demanded never to&nbsp;
be alone with Seagal on set.

00:21:49.043 --> 00:21:55.561
"The tough New Yorker in me just inwardly was&nbsp;
laughing because it was so awful and hilarious."

00:21:56.220 --> 00:22:00.720
Seagal has faced accusations of sexual&nbsp;
misconduct throughout his career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:06.360
ranging from harassment to assault to even sex&nbsp;
trafficking. In one instance, images surfaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.360 --> 00:22:11.700
of the actor groping his Under Siege 2 co-star&nbsp;
Katherine Heigl, who was only 16 at the time.

00:22:11.700 --> 00:22:14.340
"He said, 'You know, Katie, I've got&nbsp;
girlfriends your age.' And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.340 --> 00:22:18.480
'Isn't that illegal?' And he said, 'They&nbsp;
don't seem to mind.' And I said, 'Mom!'"

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:25.740
In 1991, actress Raenne Malone, who was Seagal's&nbsp;
assistant on Out for Justice, and three other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:30.780
women accused him of sexual harassment. Warner&nbsp;
Brothers ultimately reportedly paid them $50,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:36.000
each. Other incidents have also made headlines&nbsp;
without resulting in any legal consequences.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:40.860
Then in 2017, the #MeToo movement cast&nbsp;
a light on serial predators in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.860 --> 00:22:44.940
entertainment industry. Suddenly, decades&nbsp;
of allegations against Seagal could no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.940 --> 00:22:49.380
longer be overlooked. Portia de Rossi came&nbsp;
forward with casting couch horror stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:55.380
while model Faviola Dadis and actor Regina Simons&nbsp;
accused Seagal of rape. The Los Angeles County&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:55.380 --> 00:23:00.360
District Attorney's office investigated Dadis&nbsp;
and Simons' claims in 2018, though no charges&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.360 --> 00:23:06.180
resulted. In total, more than a dozen women&nbsp;
have reportedly accused Seagal of sex crimes.

